            <ArticleTitle>Obesity: Cognitive impairment and the failure to 'eat right'.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>R685-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.cub.2014.06.031</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0960-9822(14)00747-7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A recent study has found that obese women (but not men) have difficulty inhibiting food-rewarded, but not money-rewarded, appetitive behaviour, suggesting that obesity is associated with cognitive deficits that could selectively promote food intake, perhaps in a sex-dependent manner.</AbstractText>
